:"2026-11-08","version":"Exported 8th November 2026"},{"level":"permit","label":"Import permit needed","detail":"Import: Controlled animal, import permitted (approved travelling wildlife exhibitions only), import permit required, high security requirements. Keeping: Travelling wildlife exhibition permit required","src":"Requirements for importing and keeping wildlife in Tasmania (2017)","url":"https://nre.tas.gov.au/Documents/Requirements%20for%20importing%20and%20keeping%20wildlife%20in%20Tasmania.pdf","checked":"2026-11-08"},{"level":"permit","label":"Import permit needed","detail":"Import: permitted (approved travelling wildlife exhibitions only), import permit required, high security requirements. Keeping: permit required","src":"Requirements for importing and keeping wildlife in Tasmania (2017)","url":"https://nre.tas.gov.au/Documents/Requirements%20for%20importing%20and%20keeping%20wildlife%20in%20Tasmania.pdf","checked":"2026-11-08"},{"level":"permit","label":"Licence needed (Class 2)","detail":"QLD Animals Regulation: Class 2 animals (listed as Ornithoptera spp.)","src":"Nature Conservation (Animals) Regulation 2020, Schedule 3","url":"https://www.legislation.qld.gov.au/view/whole/html/inforce/current/sl-2020-0136","checked":"2026-11-08"},{"level":"check","label":"Check with NRE Tasmania","detail":"Import: security requirements Controlled animal, import permitted (approved travelling. Keeping: Travelling wildlife exhibition permit required","src":"Requirements for importing and keeping wildlife in Tasmania (2017)","url":"https://nre.tas.gov.au/Documents/Requirements%20for%20importing%20and%20keeping%20wildlife%20in%20Tasmania.pdf","checked":"2026-11-08"}],"guide":[{"n":"Black-headed bull ant","sci":"Myrmecia nigriceps","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers ~2 cm","care":3,"temp":"Aggressive, excellent eyesight.","setup":"Escape-proof formicarium with outworld; feed insects and sugar.","note":"Very painful sting that can cause allergic reactions.","flags":"d","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/myrmecia-nigriceps.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-headed_Bull-Ant_%28Myrmecia_nigriceps%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Green-head ant","sci":"Rhytidoponera metallica","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers ~6 mm","care":2,"temp":"Bold forager.","setup":"Formicarium with a moist area.","note":"Common across Australia. Metallic green sheen. Stings.","flags":"d","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/rhytidoponera-metallica.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhytidoponera_metallica_body_shot.jpg"}},{"n":"Meat ant","sci":"Iridomyrmex purpureus","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers 6-12 mm","care":3,"temp":"Very active, huge colonies.","setup":"Large setup; colonies grow fast.","note":"Builds big gravel-covered mounds.","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/iridomyrmex-purpureus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meat_eater_ant_feeding_on_honey02.jpg"}},{"n":"Banded sugar ant","sci":"Camponotus consobrinus","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers 5-15 mm","care":1,"temp":"Calm, nocturnal.","setup":"Test-tube start, then a formicarium.","note":"A classic beginner ant across Australia.","flags":"x","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/camponotus-consobrinus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black-headed_sugar_ant.jpg"}},{"n":"Green tree ant","sci":"Oecophylla smaragdina","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers 5-10 mm","care":3,"temp":"Aggressive, bites.","setup":"Needs live plants to weave leaf nests; tropical warmth.","note":"Northern WA native. Hard to keep outside the tropics.","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/oecophylla-smaragdina.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Weaver_Ant%2C_Oecophylla_smaragdina.jpg"}},{"n":"Red imported fire ant","sci":"Solenopsis invicta","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers 2-6 mm","care":1,"temp":"Aggressive, painful stings.","setup":"Not allowed.","note":"A national biosecurity emergency. Report sightings to DPIRD.","flags":"d","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":2},"photo":{"f":"img/solenopsis-invicta.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fire_ants_01.jpg"}},{"n":"Argentine ant","sci":"Linepithema humile","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers 2-3 mm","care":1,"temp":"Invasive.","setup":"Not allowed.","note":"Invasive pest already in parts of WA.","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/linepithema-humile.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linepithema_Argentine_ant.jpg"}},{"n":"Yellow crazy ant","sci":"Anoplolepis gracilipes","fam":"Formicidae","size":"Workers ~4 mm","care":1,"temp":"Invasive.","setup":"Not allowed.","note":"One of the world's worst invasive species.","sub":"ants","st":{"WA":2},"photo":{"f":"img/anoplolepis-gracilipes.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starr_050610_2340_anoplolepis_gracilipes.jpg"}},{"n":"Mueller's stag beetle","sci":"Phalacrognathus muelleri","fam":"Lucanidae","size":"Up to 7 cm","care":2,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Larvae need rotting-wood substrate for 1-2 years.","note":"Stunning rainbow-metallic beetle from north Queensland.","flags":"r,x","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/phalacrognathus-muelleri.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phalacrognathus_muelleri_sjh.jpg"}},{"n":"Rhinoceros beetle","sci":"Xylotrupes ulysses","fam":"Scarabaeidae","size":"Up to 6 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile, hisses when handled.","setup":"Deep compost and rotting wood for larvae; fruit for adults.","note":"Only males have the horns.","flags":"x","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/xylotrupes-ulysses.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhinoceros_Beetle_Top.JPG"}},{"n":"Superworm","sci":"Zophobas atratus","fam":"Tenebrionidae","size":"Larvae ~5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Tub with bran and vegetable scraps.","note":"Popular reptile feeder.","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/zophobas-atratus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zophobas_morio.jpg"}},{"n":"Mealworm","sci":"Tenebrio molitor","fam":"Tenebrionidae","size":"Larvae ~2.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Tub with bran or oats and vegetable scraps.","note":"Easy feeder colony.","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/tenebrio-molitor.png","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tenebrio_molitor_Linn%C3%A9%2C_1758_%2832842137535%29.png"}},{"n":"Dark mealworm","sci":"Tenebrio obscurus","fam":"Tenebrionidae","size":"Larvae ~2.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Not allowed.","note":"Looks like the common mealworm but is prohibited in WA.","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":2},"photo":{"f":"img/tenebrio-obscurus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkler_Mehlk%C3%A4fer_Tenebrio_obscurus_2557.jpg"}},{"n":"Hide beetle","sci":"Dermestes maculatus","fam":"Dermestidae","size":"~8 mm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless to people.","setup":"Sealed tub; used to clean skulls and bones.","note":"Escapees damage hides, wool and museum specimens.","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/dermestes-maculatus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dermestes_maculatus_DeGeer%2C_1774.jpg"}},{"n":"Spiny leaf insect","sci":"Extatosoma tiaratum","fam":"Phasmatidae","size":"Females up to 20 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile; curls its tail like a scorpion.","setup":"Tall mesh enclosure, fresh eucalyptus or rose leaves.","note":"The most popular Aussie stick insect. Females can breed without males.","flags":"x","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/extatosoma-tiaratum.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_Walking_Stick.jpg"}},{"n":"Goliath stick insect","sci":"Eurycnema goliath","fam":"Phasmatidae","size":"Up to 25 cm","care":2,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Very tall enclosure, fresh eucalyptus.","note":"One of Australia's biggest insects. Flashes red wings when disturbed.","flags":"x","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/eurycnema-goliath.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurycnema_goliath%2C_Adult_Female%2C_UK_CB%2C_186mm.jpg"}},{"n":"Titan stick insect","sci":"Acrophylla titan","fam":"Phasmatidae","size":"Up to 26 cm","care":2,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Very tall enclosure, fresh eucalyptus.","note":"Among the longest insects in Australia.","flags":"x","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0,"TAS":3},"photo":{"f":"img/acrophylla-titan.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acrophylla_titan_pinned.JPG"}},{"n":"Giant rainforest mantis","sci":"Hierodula majuscula","fam":"Mantidae","size":"Up to 10 cm","care":2,"temp":"Predator; keep one per enclosure.","setup":"Tall enclosure, live insects, misting.","note":"Australia's largest mantis.","flags":"r","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/hierodula-majuscula.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_rainforest_mantis%2C_Candlenut_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_379196017.jpg"}},{"n":"Purple-winged mantis","sci":"Tenodera australasiae","fam":"Mantidae","size":"Up to 9 cm","care":1,"temp":"Predator; keep one per enclosure.","setup":"Tall enclosure, live insects.","note":"Long, slender mantis found across Australia.","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/tenodera-australasiae.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tenodera_australasiae.jpg"}},{"n":"Garden mantis","sci":"Orthodera ministralis","fam":"Mantidae","size":"~4 cm","care":1,"temp":"Predator; keep one per enclosure.","setup":"Small mesh enclosure, live flies.","note":"The common bright green garden mantis.","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/orthodera-ministralis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orthodera_ministralis.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant burrowing cockroach","sci":"Macropanesthia rhinoceros","fam":"Blaberidae","size":"Up to 8 cm, 30 g","care":1,"temp":"Docile, can live 10 years.","setup":"Deep dry sand and soil, dried eucalyptus leaves.","note":"The world's heaviest cockroach. A popular pet, not a pest.","flags":"x","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/macropanesthia-rhinoceros.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MP_-_Macropanesthia_rhinoceros_2.jpg"}},{"n":"Wood cockroach","sci":"Panesthia cribrata","fam":"Blaberidae","size":"~4 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Rotting wood in a damp tub.","note":"Native wood-eater, harmless.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/panesthia-cribrata.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panesthia_cribrata_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_87579939_on_28_October_2022.jpg"}},{"n":"Dubia roach","sci":"Blaptica dubia","fam":"Blaberidae","size":"~4 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless, can't climb glass.","setup":"Warm tub with egg cartons.","note":"Popular reptile feeder overseas. Check interstate rules before buying.","flags":"x","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/blaptica-dubia.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dubia-cockroach-female-near-ruler.jpg"}},{"n":"Turkistan roach","sci":"Shelfordella lateralis","fam":"Blattidae","size":"~2.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Fast.","setup":"Warm tub with egg cartons.","note":"Feeder roach. Not on the WA Organism List.","flags":"x","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/shelfordella-lateralis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkestan_Cockroach%2C_Leo_Nature_Trail%2C_Malibu%2C_CA%2C_US_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_548136297.jpg"}},{"n":"Madagascar hissing cockroach","sci":"Gromphadorhina portentosa","fam":"Blaberidae","size":"5-7 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile, hisses.","setup":"Warm tub with wood and fruit.","note":"Very popular overseas. Not on the WA Organism List.","flags":"x","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/gromphadorhina-portentosa.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_Madagascar_hissing_cockroach.JPG"}},{"n":"House cricket","sci":"Acheta domesticus","fam":"Gryllidae","size":"~2 cm","care":1,"temp":"Noisy at night.","setup":"Warm tub with egg cartons and moist food.","note":"The standard feeder cricket.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/acheta-domesticus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acheta_domesticus%2C_adultes_Weibchen.jpg"}},{"n":"Black field cricket","sci":"Teleogryllus commodus","fam":"Gryllidae","size":"~2.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Noisy.","setup":"Tub with egg cartons and moist food.","note":"Native feeder cricket.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/teleogryllus-commodus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field-cricket-20070325-004.jpg"}},{"n":"Silkworm","sci":"Bombyx mori","fam":"Bombycidae","size":"Larvae ~7 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Container with fresh mulberry leaves.","note":"Classic school pet.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/bombyx-mori.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pairedmoths.jpg"}},{"n":"Greater wax moth (waxworm)","sci":"Galleria mellonella","fam":"Pyralidae","size":"Larvae ~2.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Warm jar with wax-based food.","note":"Feeder larvae. A beehive pest, so never let them near bees.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":4},"photo":{"f":"img/galleria-mellonella.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galleria_mellonella_1.jpg"}},{"n":"Black soldier fly","sci":"Hermetia illucens","fam":"Stratiomyidae","size":"Larvae ~2 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless, adults don't bite.","setup":"Compost bin.","note":"Larvae turn food scraps into feeder food.","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/hermetia-illucens.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermetia_illucens_MHNT_Fronton.jpg"}},{"n":"Queensland whistling tarantula","sci":"Phlogius crassipes","fam":"Theraphosidae","size":"Leg span up to 16 cm","care":2,"temp":"Defensive, strong bite.","setup":"Deep moist substrate for burrowing.","note":"Australia's largest spider. Its bite can kill dogs.","flags":"d","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/phlogius-crassipes.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selenocosmia_crassipes.jpg"}},{"n":"Golden whistling tarantula","sci":"Selenocosmia stirlingi","fam":"Theraphosidae","size":"Leg span ~12 cm","care":2,"temp":"Defensive.","setup":"Deep dry substrate for burrowing.","note":"Inland Australian tarantula, including WA.","flags":"d","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/selenocosmia-stirlingi.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selenocosmia_stirlingi_-_Christopher_Watson.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant huntsman","sci":"Holconia immanis","fam":"Sparassidae","size":"Leg span ~15 cm","care":1,"temp":"Fast, rarely bites.","setup":"Tall enclosure with bark to hide under.","note":"Big but harmless.","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":1,"TAS":5},"photo":{"f":"img/holconia-immanis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grey_Huntsman_spider_-_Holconia_immanis.jpg"}},{"n":"Redback spider","sci":"Latrodectus hasselti","fam":"Theridiidae","size":"Female ~1 cm","care":1,"temp":"Shy, venomous.","setup":"Small secure enclosure.","note":"Medically significant bite. Not a beginner pet.","flags":"d,x","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/latrodectus-hasselti.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latrodectus_hasselti_close.jpg"}},{"n":"Sydney funnel-web","sci":"Atrax robustus","fam":"Atracidae","size":"Body up to 5 cm","care":3,"temp":"Very defensive.","setup":"Secure, humid enclosure.","note":"One of the world's most dangerous spiders.","flags":"d,x","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":1,"TAS":6},"photo":{"f":"img/atrax-robustus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atrax_Robustus.jpg"}},{"n":"Eastern mouse spider","sci":"Missulena bradleyi","fam":"Actinopodidae","size":"Body 1-3 cm","care":2,"temp":"Defensive.","setup":"Deep substrate for burrowing.","note":"Males have a bright red head. Potentially dangerous bite.","flags":"d,x","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/missulena-bradleyi.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mouse_spider.jpg"}},{"n":"Mexican red-knee tarantula","sci":"Brachypelma hamorii","fam":"Theraphosidae","size":"Leg span ~14 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Dry enclosure with a hide.","note":"A classic pet overseas. Exotic tarantulas can't legally be imported into Australia.","sub":"spiders","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/brachypelma-hamorii.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brachypelma_smithi_2009_G09.jpg"}},{"n":"Black rock scorpion","sci":"Urodacus manicatus","fam":"Urodacidae","size":"~6 cm","care":1,"temp":"Shy.","setup":"Flat rocks over dry substrate.","note":"Mild sting.","sub":"scorpions","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/urodacus-manicatus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urodacus_manicatus2.jpg"}},{"n":"Desert scorpion","sci":"Urodacus yaschenkoi","fam":"Urodacidae","size":"~8 cm","care":1,"temp":"Burrower.","setup":"Deep sand.","note":"Inland deserts, including WA.","sub":"scorpions","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/urodacus-yaschenkoi.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urodacus_yaschenkoi_%2810.3897-evolsyst.3.37464%29_Figure_64_%28cropped%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Flinders Ranges scorpion","sci":"Urodacus elongatus","fam":"Urodacidae","size":"~12 cm","care":1,"temp":"Calm.","setup":"Rocky, dry enclosure.","note":"One of Australia's largest scorpions.","flags":"x","sub":"scorpions","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/urodacus-elongatus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urodacus_elongatus_%2824328138827%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Rainforest scorpion","sci":"Liocheles waigiensis","fam":"Hormuridae","size":"~4 cm","care":1,"temp":"Calm.","setup":"Humid enclosure with bark.","note":"Flat-bodied tropical scorpion.","sub":"scorpions","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/liocheles-waigiensis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hormurus_waigiensis_%2810.3897-evolsyst.3.37464%29_Figure_28_%28cropped%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Emperor scorpion","sci":"Pandinus imperator","fam":"Scorpionidae","size":"Up to 20 cm","care":1,"temp":"Docile.","setup":"Warm, humid enclosure.","note":"A classic pet overseas, not in Australia.","sub":"scorpions","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/pandinus-imperator.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_Emperor_Scorpion.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant centipede","sci":"Ethmostigmus rubripes","fam":"Scolopendridae","size":"Up to 16 cm","care":2,"temp":"Fast and aggressive.","setup":"Secure, escape-proof enclosure with damp substrate.","note":"Very painful bite.","flags":"d,r","sub":"others","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/ethmostigmus-rubripes.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethmostigmus_rubripes_%2839153673691%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Portuguese millipede","sci":"Ommatoiulus moreleti","fam":"Julidae","size":"~4 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Not recommended.","note":"An invasive pest that swarms into houses.","sub":"others","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/ommatoiulus-moreleti.png","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ommatoiulusmoreletii.png"}},{"n":"Common rough slater","sci":"Porcellio scaber","fam":"Porcellionidae","size":"~1.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Damp tub with leaf litter.","note":"Clean-up crew for bioactive enclosures.","flags":"r","sub":"others","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/porcellio-scaber.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porcellio_scaber_%28AU%29-left_01.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant slater","sci":"Porcellio laevis","fam":"Porcellionidae","size":"~2 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless.","setup":"Damp tub with leaf litter.","note":"Bioactive clean-up crew.","flags":"r","sub":"others","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/porcellio-laevis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porcellio_laevis_-_Bouxweerd20090527_897.jpg"}},{"n":"Common pill bug","sci":"Armadillidium vulgare","fam":"Armadillidiidae","size":"~1.5 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless, rolls into a ball.","setup":"Damp tub with leaf litter.","note":"Not on the WA Organism List.","flags":"r","sub":"others","st":{"WA":1},"photo":{"f":"img/armadillidium-vulgare.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armadillidium_vulgare_male.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant African land snail","sci":"Achatina fulica","fam":"Achatinidae","size":"Up to 20 cm","care":1,"temp":"Harmless to people.","setup":"Not allowed.","note":"One of the world's worst pests. Report any sighting.","sub":"others","st":{"WA":2},"photo":{"f":"img/achatina-fulica.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snail_in_Ubud%2C_Bali%2C_2010_%281%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Adisura marginalis","sci":"Adisura marginalis","note":"Adisura marginalis is a species of moth of the family Noctuidae. It is found in Myanmar, India, Malaysia, the Philippines, Thailand, Vietnam and Indonesia, as well as the Northern Territory, Queensland and Western Australia in Australia.","w":"Adisura marginalis","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0}},{"n":"Bean weevil","sci":"Acanthoscelides obtectus","note":"Acanthoscelides obtectus, the bean weevil, is a species of bruchid beetle. The species was described in 1831 by Thomas Say.","w":"Acanthoscelides obtectus","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/acanthoscelides-obtectus.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acanthoscelides_obtectus_%28Say%2C_1831%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Brown cutworm","sci":"Agrotis munda","note":"Agrotis munda, the brown cutworm or pink cutworm, is a noctuid moth. It is endemic to Australia. It is present in New Zealand.","w":"Agrotis munda","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/agrotis-munda.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrotis_munda_male_LCR.jpg"}},{"n":"Cairns birdwing","sci":"Ornithoptera euphorion","note":"Ornithoptera euphorion, the Cairns birdwing, is a species of birdwing butterfly endemic to Queensland, and is Australia's largest endemic butterfly species. Other common names include Cooktown birdwing and northern birdwing.","w":"Ornithoptera euphorion","auto":"2026-11-09","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0,"QLD":7},"photo":{"f":"img/ornithoptera-euphorion.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MG_3164.jpg"}},{"n":"Castor semi-looper","sci":"Achaea janata","note":"Achaea janata, the castor semi-looper or croton caterpillar, is an erebid moth, the caterpillars of which are termed 'semi-loopers' due to their mode of locomotion. It is found from the Indo-Australian tropics and subtropics, extending south to New Zealand and east through the Pacific archipelagoes to Easter Island.","w":"Achaea janata","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/achaea-janata.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achaea_janata.jpg"}},{"n":"Children's stick insect","sci":"Tropidoderus childrenii","note":"Tropidoderus childrenii, the Children's stick insect, was first described in 1833 by Gray. The species is named after John George Children.","w":"Tropidoderus childrenii","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0,"TAS":5},"photo":{"f":"img/tropidoderus-childrenii.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tropidoderus_childrenii-1.jpg"}},{"n":"Convolvulus hawk-moth","sci":"Agrius convolvuli","note":"Agrius convolvuli, the convolvulus hawk-moth, is a large hawk-moth. It is common throughout Europe, Asia, Africa, Australia and New Zealand, partly as a migrant. In New Zealand, it is also known as the kumara moth, and in the Māori language as hīhue.","w":"Agrius convolvuli","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/agrius-convolvuli.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrius_convolvuli_from_Sri_Lanka.jpg"}},{"n":"Coral tree moth","sci":"Agathodes ostentalis","note":"Agathodes ostentalis, the coral tree moth, is a species of moth of the family Crambidae described by Carl Geyer in 1837. It is found from India to Indonesia, including Hong Kong, Japan, Thailand and in Australia in New South Wales and Queensland.","w":"Agathodes ostentalis","auto":"2026-11-09","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/agathodes-ostentalis.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agathodes_ostentalis_India.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant green slantface","sci":"Acrida conica","note":"Acrida conica, the giant green slantface, is a species of grasshopper found in Australia and New Guinea. It was originally described in 1781 as Truxalis conicus.","w":"Acrida conica","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"roaches","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/acrida-conica.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acrida_Conica.jpg"}},{"n":"Giant northern stick insect","sci":"Acrophylla wuelfingi","note":"Acrophylla wuelfingi, also commonly known as the giant northern stick insect or Wülfing's stick-insect, is a species of stick insect belonging to the taxonomic tribe Phasmatini. They live in the northern east-coast regions of Queensland, Australia.","w":"Acrophylla wuelfingi","auto":"2026-11-09","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":0,"TAS":8}},{"n":"Spur legged phasmid","sci":"Didymuria violescens","note":"Didymuria violescens, the spur legged phasmid, also known as the violet-winged stick insect, or violet-winged phasma, is a common phasmid native to Australia.","w":"Didymuria violescens","auto":"2026-11-09","sub":"phasmids","st":{"WA":2,"TAS":5},"photo":{"f":"img/didymuria-violescens.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Natural_history_of_Victoria_%28Pl._79%29_%285998829322%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Tawny coster","sci":"Acraea terpsicore","note":"Acraea terpsicore, the tawny coster, is a small, 53–64 millimetres (2.1–2.5 in), leathery-winged butterfly common in grassland and scrub habitats. It belongs to the Nymphalidae or brush-footed butterfly family. It has a weak fluttery flight. It is avoided by most insect predators.","w":"Acraea terpsicore","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/acraea-terpsicore.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Open_wing_position_of_Acraea_terpsicore_Linnaeus%2C_1758_%E2%80%93_Tawny_Coster.jpg"}},{"n":"Two-spot ladybird","sci":"Adalia bipunctata","note":"Adalia bipunctata, the two-spot ladybird, two-spotted ladybug or two-spotted lady beetle, is a carnivorous beetle of the family Coccinellidae that is found throughout the holarctic region. It is very common in western and central Europe.","w":"Adalia bipunctata","auto":"2026-11-08","sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/adalia-bipunctata.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adalia_bipunctata_%282007-04-08%29.jpg"}},{"n":"Dark sword-grass","sci":"Agrotis ipsilon","note":"Agrotis ipsilon, the dark sword-grass, ipsilon dart, black cutworm, greasy cutworm or floodplain cutworm, is a small noctuid moth found worldwide. The moth gets its scientific name from black markings on its forewings shaped like the letter \"Y\" or the Greek letter upsilon.","w":"Agrotis ipsilon","auto":"2026-11-08","pest":1,"sub":"butterflies","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/agrotis-ipsilon.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrotis_ipsilon_aneituma.jpg"}},{"n":"Red-headed cockchafer","sci":"Adoryphorus coulonii","note":"The red-headed cockchafer or red-headed pasture cockchafer is a species of Australian scarab beetle in the genus Adoryphorus. It is a pasture pest in Victoria, New South Wales, South Australia and Tasmania. It has become naturalised in Canterbury, New Zealand, where it was first recorded in 1963.","w":"Red-headed cockchafer","auto":"2026-11-08","pest":1,"sub":"beetles","st":{"WA":0},"photo":{"f":"img/adoryphorus-coulonii.jpg","c":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adoryphonus_couloni_2.jpg"}}]}